1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,800
Your employer uses Microsoft 365, and you
plan your work in Microsoft To Do on your

2
00:00:05,850 --> 00:00:07,050
phone.

3
00:00:08,350 --> 00:00:12,297
On your Linux desktop, Merkzettel brings
these tasks into a native Plasma app.

4
00:00:12,597 --> 00:00:16,266
After installing it from Flathub or the
AUR, it greets you with this page.

5
00:00:20,350 --> 00:00:23,415
Sign in opens your browser with
Microsoft's own sign-in page.

6
00:00:23,715 --> 00:00:25,921
There you enter your work address and your
password.

7
00:00:31,350 --> 00:00:36,075
Merkzettel lists exactly what it asks for:
your tasks, your name, and to stay signed

8
00:00:36,125 --> 00:00:36,546
in.

9
00:00:36,596 --> 00:00:38,848
It does not touch mail, calendar or files.

10
00:00:43,350 --> 00:00:46,334
Some organisations allow only apps
approved by IT.

11
00:00:46,634 --> 00:00:50,816
Microsoft then asks for admin approval,
and the documentation explains what to
tell

12
00:00:50,866 --> 00:00:52,066
your IT department.

13
00:00:56,350 --> 00:00:59,322
For this video, we use the demo data
instead of a real account.

14
00:00:59,622 --> 00:01:03,536
After signing in, your lists appear in the
sidebar, including lists colleagues share

15
00:01:03,586 --> 00:01:04,786
with you.

16
00:01:09,350 --> 00:01:11,161
Changes sync in both directions.

17
00:01:11,461 --> 00:01:15,255
What you tick off here is done on your
phone, and new tasks from your phone show
up

18
00:01:15,305 --> 00:01:16,505
here.

19
00:01:20,350 --> 00:01:22,289
Merkzettel uses one account at a time.

20
00:01:22,589 --> 00:01:24,934
To switch, sign out and sign in with the
other one.

21
00:01:29,350 --> 00:01:31,359
That is how you use your work account with
Merkzettel.

